Artix Love
==========

I don't want to run it, but I want to love it.

That is the whole rationale. OpenRC is a pain in the ass, which is part of why it is not the daily
driver here — and it is also worth supporting properly. So rather than install it, this package gets
built and tested on it: Artix ships four inits (OpenRC, runit, s6, dinit) and there is no reason a
desktop application should care which one is supervising the machine.

This patch is what a cold build on Artix actually required. The suite would not pass in a container,
and the reason had nothing to do with init systems: a test asserted that the pointer position becomes
known after a mouse move, without first checking that this host can move a mouse at all. On a
container, on CI, or with no ydotoold running, the move cannot happen — so the assertion was really
"this machine has a pointer", which is the environment talking, not the contract.

The fix asks the capability report instead of assuming, and asserts something real either way:

  * input available  -> after a successful move the position is known
  * no input backend -> the move cannot have happened, so a position must NOT be claimed

Both branches are meaningful; neither is a skip.

Found by: building the package in artixlinux/artixlinux on a machine that is not mine.
Companion document: ARTIX.md. OpenRC service: contrib/ydotoold.openrc.

---
--- a/crates/quixi-platform/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/quixi-platform/src/lib.rs
@@ -2189,13 +2189,27 @@
         let fresh = CuSessionState::default();
         assert!(!fresh.cursor_known, "a new session must not claim a known cursor position");
 
-        // Moving the mouse makes it known by construction, because we choose the destination.
+        // A successful move makes the position known by construction, because we choose the
+        // destination. That only means anything on a host that can actually inject input: in a
+        // container, on CI, or with no ydotoold running, the move cannot happen at all, and
+        // asserting "the position is known" would be asserting that this machine has a mouse.
+        //
+        // The capability report already answers this, so ask it rather than assuming. This is the
+        // same defect as the other portability fixes in this suite: the test was measuring the
+        // environment it happened to run in.
         CuSessionState::default();
         ComputerUse::mouse_move(123.0, 456.0);
-        assert!(
-            ComputerUse::cursor_position_available(),
-            "after a move the position is known"
-        );
+        if crate::linux::capabilities().input_synthesis {
+            assert!(
+                ComputerUse::cursor_position_available(),
+                "after a move that succeeded, the position is known"
+            );
+        } else {
+            assert!(
+                !ComputerUse::cursor_position_available(),
+                "with no input backend the move cannot happen, so the position must not be claimed"
+            );
+        }
         // Deliberately NOT asserting the exact coordinate here.
         //
         // `get_cursor_position()` reads the OS when a display is present, and on XWayland that
